Q: How can I return the name of a variable by entering its value? A: <p>If you're willing to change your approach slightly:</p>

<pre><code>var Data = new Object();
Data["myGoal"] = "answer";
// ...

// Get response from prompt...
var response = /* ... */;

for (var key in Data)
{
    if (Data[key] == response)
    {
        alert(key);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: get rust default stack size at runtime A: <p><a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/thread/index.html#stack-size" rel="nofollow noreferrer">std::thread - Stack Size</a></p>
<blockquote>
<h3>Stack Size</h3>
<p>The default stack size is platform-dependent and subject to change. Currently, it is 2 MiB on all Tier-1 platforms.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It doesn't appear what you want is currently offered in the API. I would recommend using the following code as an approximation:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>static DEFAULT_STACK_SIZE: LazyLock&lt;usize&gt; = LazyLock::new(|| {
    // https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/a72e2a71d8fbcbc46cdd18784e2ab2c32cbd9c93/library/std/src/sys/thread/unix.rs#L26
    // https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/a72e2a71d8fbcbc46cdd18784e2ab2c32cbd9c93/library/std/src/sys/thread/windows.rs#L14
    const DEFAULT_MIN_STACK_SIZE: usize = 2 * 1024 * 1024;

    // https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/a72e2a71d8fbcbc46cdd18784e2ab2c32cbd9c93/library/std/src/thread/lifecycle.rs#L37-L39
    std::env::var_os(&quot;RUST_MIN_STACK&quot;)
        .and_then(|s| s.to_str().and_then(|s| s.parse().ok()))
        .unwrap_or(DEFAULT_MIN_STACK_SIZE)
});
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&amp;mode=debug&amp;edition=2024&amp;gist=1a112f86d888770644abf2c533d27155" rel="nofollow noreferrer">playground</a></p>
<p>You might want to actually set the <code>stack_size</code> of the threads to make it consistent:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>const STACK_SIZE: usize = 2 * 1024 * 1024;
std::thread::Builder::new().stack_size(STACK_SIZE).spawn(...)
</code></pre>

Q: How to test promises with Mocha A: <p>Mocha has built-in Promise support. If you return a promise from a test case, Mocha will wait for it:</p>
<pre><code>it('does something asynchronous', function() { // note: no `done` argument
  return getSomePromise().then(function(value) {
    expect(value).to.equal('foo');
  });
});
</code></pre>
<p>I have spelled out the promise logic explicitly here, but usually, your test will be more readable if you use <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>. An equivalent, more idiomatic version of the code above would be:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>it('does something asynchronous', async function() {
  const value = await getSomePromise();
  expect(value).to.equal('foo');
});
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>There is also the <a href="https://github.com/domenic/chai-as-promised" rel="nofollow noreferrer">chai-as-promised</a> library, which gives you an <code>eventually</code> property to test promise objects:</p>
<pre><code>it('does something asynchronous', function() {
  return expect(getSomePromise()).to.eventually.equal('foo');
});

it('fails asynchronously', function() {
  return expect(getAnotherPromise()).to.be.rejectedWith(Error, /some message/);
});
</code></pre>
<p>That said, with modern <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> syntax, I haven't found much need for this anymore.</p>

Q: Could we save delegates in a file (C#)? A: <p>Leaving aside security concerns that storing a delegate in a file may cause, technicallywise you can save its type information and restore back to an instance, as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>[Serializable]
public struct ActionInfo
{
    [Serializable]
    public struct TypeInfo
    {
        public string Assembly { get; }
        public string Class { get; }

        public TypeInfo(Type type)
        {
            Assembly = type.Assembly.FullName;
            Class = type.FullName;
        }

        public Type Load()
        {
            var assembly = AppDomain.CurrentDomain.Load(Assembly);
            return assembly.GetType(Class);
        }
    }

    public TypeInfo ActionType { get; }
    public string ActionName { get; }
    public TypeInfo[] ActionParameters { get; }
    public BindingFlags ActionBindingFlags { get; }
    public TypeInfo DelegateType { get; }

    public ActionInfo(Delegate action)
    {
        var method = action.Method;

        if (!method.IsStatic)
            throw new ArgumentException(&quot;Action must be a static method&quot;);

        ActionType = new(method.DeclaringType);
        ActionName = method.Name;
        ActionParameters = method.GetParameters().Select(parameter =&gt; new TypeInfo(parameter.ParameterType)).ToArray();
        ActionBindingFlags = BindingFlags.Static | (method.IsPublic ? BindingFlags.Public : BindingFlags.NonPublic);

        DelegateType = new(action.GetType());
    }

    public Delegate ToAction()
    {
        var actionClass = ActionType.Load();
        var actionParams = ActionParameters.Select(param =&gt; param.Load()).ToArray();
        var actionMethod = actionClass.GetMethod(ActionName, ActionBindingFlags, null, actionParams, null);

        var delegateClass = DelegateType.Load();

        return Delegate.CreateDelegate(delegateClass, actionMethod);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The implementation supports only static methods but can be easily adapted for instance ones.</p>
<p>Usage:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Method(parameters);
...
var info = new ActionInfo(Method);
SaveToFile(info);
...
var info = ReadFromFile();
var action = info.ToAction();
action.Method.Invoke(null, parameters)
</code></pre>

Q: ASP.NET Controls Don&#39;t Span Bootstrap Columns A: <p>OK, let's assume this GridView (it renders a table).</p>
<pre><code>&lt;asp:GridView ID=&quot;GVHotels&quot; runat=&quot;server&quot; AutoGenerateColumns=&quot;False&quot;
DataKeyNames=&quot;ID&quot; 
    CssClass=&quot;table table-hover&quot;
Width=&quot;50%&quot;&gt;
&lt;Columns&gt;
    &lt;asp:BoundField DataField=&quot;FirstName&quot; HeaderText=&quot;FirstName&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;asp:BoundField DataField=&quot;LastName&quot; HeaderText=&quot;LastName&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;asp:BoundField DataField=&quot;City&quot; HeaderText=&quot;City&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;asp:TemplateField HeaderText=&quot;Hotel Name&quot;&gt;
        &lt;ItemTemplate&gt;
            &lt;asp:TextBox ID=&quot;txtHotel&quot; runat=&quot;server&quot;
                Text = '&lt;%# Eval(&quot;HotelName&quot;) %&gt;'
                &gt;
            &lt;/asp:TextBox&gt;
        &lt;/ItemTemplate&gt;
    &lt;/asp:TemplateField&gt;

    &lt;asp:TemplateField ItemStyle-HorizontalAlign=&quot;Center&quot;&gt;
        &lt;ItemTemplate&gt;
            &lt;asp:ImageButton ID=&quot;cmdDel&quot; runat=&quot;server&quot;
                OnClick=&quot;cmdDel_Click&quot;
                OnClientClick=&quot;return mydelete(this)&quot;
                ImageUrl=&quot;~/Content/trashcan1.png&quot;
                Width=&quot;48&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/ItemTemplate&gt;
    &lt;/asp:TemplateField&gt;
&lt;/Columns&gt;
&lt;/asp:GridView&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>And note how we bootstrap the GridView (table) with this:</p>
<pre><code>    CssClass=&quot;table table-hover&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>And our code behind to load above is this:</p>
<pre><code>    Protected Sub Page_Load(ByVal sender As Object, ByVal e As System.EventArgs) Handles Me.Load

        If Not IsPostBack Then
            LoadGrid
        End If
    End Sub

    Sub LoadGrid()

        Dim strSQL As String =
            &quot;SELECT * FROM tblHotels
            WHERE Description is not null
            ORDER BY HotelName&quot;

        GVHotels.DataSource = MyRst(strSQL)
        GVHotels.DataBind()

    End Sub

</code></pre>
<p>And the result is now this:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/lGOViVi9.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/lGOViVi9.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>Note closely how the textbox in the table does not fill out the whole column.</p>
<p>So, just add to the TextBox a style to fill out 100% of that column.</p>
<p>So, say this:</p>
<pre><code>                &lt;asp:TemplateField HeaderText=&quot;Hotel Name&quot;&gt;
                    &lt;ItemTemplate&gt;
                        &lt;asp:TextBox ID=&quot;txtHotel&quot; runat=&quot;server&quot;
                            Text = '&lt;%# Eval(&quot;HotelName&quot;) %&gt;'
                             Style=&quot;width:100%&quot; &gt;
                        &lt;/asp:TextBox&gt;
                    &lt;/ItemTemplate&gt;
                &lt;/asp:TemplateField&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>And now the result is this:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/HchME0Oy.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/HchME0Oy.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>So, in most cases, just add some CSS (style) to the given control. Set the width = 100% as per above. This should result in the given control filling out the width (available space) for the given column.</p>
